message:
{{#is_alert}}Production LLM spend in the trailing 24h is over $16 — runaway territory. The busiest production day measured (2026-09-06, $6.99) was well under this; ten uncached reviews or a loop get here in an hour or two.{{/is_alert}}
{{#is_warning}}Production LLM spend in the trailing 24h is over $8 — above any production day seen so far; a glance, not a page.{{/is_warning}}

Triage on the [fleet dashboard](https://app.datadoghq.com/dashboard/bwm-uny-qqs):
- **Avg cost per model call** flat -> volume spike (who shipped a lot? a PR train is benign — 2026-09-02 was $5.56 at a normal $0.017/call).
- **Avg cost per call** stepped up -> price change: model swap, prompt growth, or a broken cache prefix (check the caching row). The companion monitor "Fleet LLM cost per call — price signal" watches this directly.
- **Cost by ml_app** names the spender; **cost by model** says what it ran on.

**Population (RC1-411).** This query excludes `service:evals` and `service:dry-run` — corpus sweeps and `measure_pr.py` runs report to LLM Obs under the same `ml_app` as production traffic, and paging on measurement work is not the job. Everything else the fleet bills is in scope, across all nine ml_apps. Measurement spend is real money and is still visible on the dashboard; it is just not page-worthy.

Metric is nanodollars; the query divides by 1e9 so thresholds read in dollars. Guardrail from RC1-349; thresholds raised by RC1-377 (2026-09-03) from $1.50/$3, then re-derived by RC1-411 (2026-09-08) from the production-only distribution: 11 days, median $1.73, p90 $5.56, max $6.99. Warn sits just above the busiest real day, alert at ~2x warn — the same rule RC1-377 used, applied to a number that no longer includes corpus runs.

